.gradle/
build/
local.properties
.superpowers/
# Harness state, like .superpowers/ above -- written by the tooling, never by the project.
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
*.iml
.idea/
*.hprof

# The Kotlin Gradle plugin writes `.kotlin/sessions/<id>.salive` during every build and removes it
# only on a clean shutdown, so any interrupted build leaves it behind. Untracked debris here is not
# harmless: `ci/mutation-probes.sh` refuses to run on a dirty tree (it reverts with
# `git checkout --`, which cannot tell a probe from real work), so without this line an interrupted
# build makes the probe list unrunnable until someone deletes a directory they did not create.
.kotlin/

# Upload-key material. NEVER tracked, in any form: not the keystore, not the properties file that
# names it, not an encrypted or base64-encoded copy in a workflow. A leaked upload key means an
# app that can be impersonated, and unlike a password it cannot be rotated for users who have
# already installed -- Play App Signing can re-key an *upload* key, but only after Google support
# has been convinced, and only for an account that still controls the app.
#
# CI reads the four values from repository secrets instead (MUPLAY_KEYSTORE_PATH,
# MUPLAY_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD, MUPLAY_KEY_ALIAS, MUPLAY_KEY_PASSWORD -- see `releaseSigningConfig` in
# build-logic). A developer puts them in `keystore.properties`, which is this list's first line.
#
# These patterns are a convenience, not the guarantee. `ConventionTest`'s
# `no keystore material is tracked by git` is the guarantee: it asks git what is in the index,
# so it also catches a file added with `git add -f` and a file this list forgot to name.
keystore.properties
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pfx
*.bks
*.pepk

# Python bytecode from the ci/ generator scripts. Written on import (ci/generate-feature-graphic.py
# imports ci/generate-launcher-icon.py by path), never by the project.
__pycache__/
